Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 08:03:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] C14, patch 5 (cld-advisor, at main's word in M24): THE
 README'S LINE ON THE LAST LANDING GIVES ITS DAY AND WHERE ITS ENTRY STANDS,
 NOT A HEADING OF THE CHANGELOG; THE PUBLIC FACE'S TEST READS THE GENERATED
 BLOCK MADE FROM A PLANTED HEADING

Main's M24: the README's line "the last landing" printed the first 150 characters of the newest changelog heading that
begins with "## S", so a heading in the working record's words reached the front page. Main's choice for the builder: a
plain title that a landing names for itself, or the line dropped.

THE CHOICE: neither quite. The line stays, and it gives what a reader can use without the heading's words: the
landing's day, read from the heading's last bracket, and where its entry stands ("its entry heads CHANGELOG.md, the
working record"). A plain title that every landing names for itself would ask a new step of every landing, which no gate
holds; a line dropped would lose the day. The generator's own words are plain.

THE GATE READS THE BLOCK. The gate public-face already reads the README whole, the generated block with it; what it could
not see is the next heading. tests/test_public_face.py gains one test: the block is made by the generator from a planted
changelog whose heading carries six inner words; the line is the day and the place, and the block counts no inner word.
The twin: the line as it stood, made from the same heading, counts them.

BEFORE: the new test fails on the generator as it stood (1 failed, 4 passed); after, 5 passed.

The README's block on this tree: "| the last landing | 2026-11-13; its entry heads `CHANGELOG.md` (the working record) |".

0 of 19.
